<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord's Prayer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Our Father, Pater Noster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lord's Prayer, also called the Our Father or Pater Noster, is a central Christian prayer attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. It survives in two versions, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Matthew and Luke, and the first-century Didache gives a form close to Matthew's. Most scholars regard it as deriving from the historical Jesus, though they debate how the two versions relate. Matthew's has seven petitions, the first three addressed to God and the other four to human needs, and it is the form used in most Christian worship. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and Protestant usage add a closing doxology, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches use their own.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lords-prayer.jpg" alt="Lord's Prayer" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Tissot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Lord's_Prayer_(Le_Pater_Noster)_-_James_Tissot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The standard edition of the Greek text opens with the address Páter hēmôn ho en toîs ouranoîs, followed by petitions that the name of God be hallowed, that his kingdom come and that his will be done on earth as in heaven. In the first part of the prayer the verbs are third-person passive imperatives, and in the last part second-person active imperatives. The Peshitta gives the prayer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, and the Clementine Vulgate of 1692 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Pater noster qui es in cælis.</p><p>The Greek word epiousion, usually rendered as daily in the request for bread, occurs nowhere else in surviving ancient Greek writing outside the two versions of the prayer. Matthew 6:12 uses the word debts, while many older English versions use trespasses and ecumenical versions often use sins, the word in Luke 11:4. Translations and scholars are divided over whether the last petition asks to be delivered from evil in general or from the evil one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>English translations began around 650 CE with the Northumbrian version. The three best known in current liturgical use are those of the 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>, the slightly modernised traditional ecumenical form, and the 1988 translation of the English Language Liturgical Consultation. All are based on Matthew's text. Most Protestants conclude with the doxology &quot;For thine is the kingdom, the power, and the glory&quot;, which resembles 1 Chronicles 29:11.</p><p>As the faith reached new peoples, the prayer was often one of the first texts translated into a language, long before the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. From the 16th century collections of its translations were used to compare languages, the first being Conrad Gessner's Mithridates, de differentiis linguarum of 1555, with 22 versions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> wrote an analysis of the prayer, reading &quot;heaven&quot; as the hearts of the righteous. The Catechism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> calls it the summary of the whole gospel. Some authors point to parallels with other texts, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the Dhammapada, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> and the Egyptian Book of the Dead.</p><p>Composers have set the prayer from Gregorian chant of the 9th and 10th centuries onward. Settings include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orlande-de-lassus">Orlando di Lasso</a>'s Pater Noster for four voices of 1573, Heinrich Schütz's Pater Noster of 1625, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a>'s Otche Nash of 1878 and Sergei Rachmaninoff's of 1910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lord's Prayer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
